## Santa Rosa Lemon Law FAQs

## What Warranty Terms Qualify Under California Lemon Law?

Any vehicle sold with a warranty could qualify under California lemon law. Warranty terms may vary by manufacturer, but most cover a vehicle for three years from the date of purchase or up to 36,000 miles. You may have a valid claim as long as the defect is first reported within the warranty terms.

## Can I File a Lemon Law Claim Without a Lawyer?

Technically, yes, but it’s not recommended. Manufacturers often deny claims filed directly by consumers, even when they are legitimate. They tend use shady tactics to avoid liability and have teams of lawyers helping them. A skilled California lemon law attorney knows how to counter these tactics to seek fair settlements.

## Is There a Deadline for Filing a Lemon Law Claim in California?

You have four years from the time the defect is discovered to file a lemon law claim, but we recommend doing it as soon as possible. Waiting too long could imply that the defect wasn’t serious enough to merit a claim. More importantly, driving a defective vehicle puts you and others at risk.

## What If I Want a Replacement Vehicle Instead of a Buyback?

You can request a replacement from the manufacturer. They may offer a vehicle of the same model or one of equivalent value. However, manufacturers are only required by law to offer a buyback - must agree to provide a replacement. We generally recommend pursuing a full buyback for a lemon vehicle.

## Does California Lemon Law Apply to Private Car Sales?

Generally, no. Private sellers aren’t required to offer warranties. This means most private vehicle sales are considered “as is.” In other words, even if your car breaks down the next day, you most likely won’t have any legal options, even with a lawyer’s help.

## How Long Will My California Lemon Law Claim Take to Settle?

Typically, lemon law claims can be resolved within a few weeks to a few months, though some can take longer. We try to settle claims within 30 days, but every case is unique. Cases may take longer depending on their complexity and the manufacturer’s response.
